.TH VIDWIZARD 1 "2026-08-20" "vidwizard 0.4.0-alpha" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vidwizard \- high-quality command-line video edits
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vidwizard
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.IR inputs ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vidwizard
is a command-line video editor.
Name a file, name the effect, get a high-quality result without a GUI.
.PP
This is
.BR 0.4.0-alpha .
.PP
Defaults keep quality high (libx264 CRF 16, Rec.709 grayscale,
max-compressed 32-bit PNG) and use every logical CPU core.
Options and input paths may appear in any order.
Several operations on one command run as one sequential filter graph
(for example crop, then grayscale, then speed) without extra flags.
Ranges are optional; omit them to apply an effect to the whole clip.
With no arguments,
.B vidwizard
prints the same help as
.B \-h
and exits 0.
.PP
Input directories expand to common video types.
.B \-v
/
.B \-\-version
print the version.
.B \-\-verbose
writes extra progress to stderr.
.SS Time ranges
Time ranges are
.IR START-END,START-END,...
An open end
.RI ( 10- ", " 1:00- ", " 5:00- )
runs to the end of the clip.
An open start
.RI ( \-20 )
runs from time zero.
Times may be seconds
.RI ( 10.5 ),
.I MM:SS
or
.IR HH:MM:SS
(fractions allowed).
Empty ranges mean the whole timeline.
.SS Crop versus zoom
.B \-\-crop
keeps a pixel rectangle.
A whole-clip crop changes the output resolution.
A
.I ranged
crop keeps that rectangle and pads the rest of the frame black; it does
.B not
zoom.
Output size stays the source size for a ranged crop.
.PP
.B \-\-zoom
fills the frame: the picture scales around a normalized center while
the output stays the source resolution.
.SS Reverse and memory
.B \-\-reverse
buffers the selected frames in memory.
That is fine for short clips; a long 4K reverse can need a lot of RAM.
.SS Default output names
With no
.BR \-o ,
outputs sit next to the input:
.IR stem_gray.ext ,
.IR stem_01.png ,
.IR stem_cut_01.ext ,
.IR stem_speed.ext ,
.IR stem_crop.ext ,
.IR stem_rev.ext ,
.IR stem_mute.ext ,
.IR stem_zoom.ext ,
.IR stem_text.ext ,
or
.I stem_edit.ext
when combining operations.
PNG index width matches the frame count (20 frames → 01..20;
1000 frames → 0001..1000).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
Printed automatically when
.B vidwizard
is invoked with no arguments.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-o ", " \-\-output " PATH"
Single output file or directory (may be given only once).
One input plus a file path uses that exact name.
Several inputs plus
.B \-o out.mp4
write
.IR out_<input>.mp4 .
A directory (existing, trailing slash, or created) receives
default-named outputs.
.TP
.BR \-\-grayscale [ =RANGES ]
High-quality Rec.709 grayscale.
Optional ranges apply the effect only in those windows.
.TP
.BR \-\-explode [ =RANGES ]
Write lossless 32-bit RGBA PNG frames with zlib level 9 and mixed
prediction.
Names are
.I stem_0001.png
with zero-pad width taken from the frame count.
.TP
.BI \-\-cut " RANGES"
Extract each range as its own video
.RI ( stem_cut_01.ext ).
.B \-\-cut
requires at least one
.IR START-END
range.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " FACTOR[:RANGES]"
Forward speed multiplier (for example 1.5, 2, 2.75).
Audio stays in pitch unless
.B \-\-mute
is given.
Optional ranges speed only those windows; the rest stays 1×.
.TP
.BI \-\-crop " GEOM[:RANGES]"
Crop
.I WxH+X+Y
or
.IR W:H:X:Y .
.I WxH
or
.I W:H
crops from the centre.
Ranged crop keeps that rectangle and pads the rest black (it does not
zoom).
See
.B Crop versus zoom
above.
.TP
.BR \-\-reverse [ =RANGES ]
Reverse the whole video, or reverse listed windows in place.
Buffers frames in RAM (see
.B Reverse and memory
above).
.TP
.BR \-\-mute [ =RANGES ]
Drop all audio, or silence the listed windows (audio stream kept).
.TP
.BI \-\-zoom " SPEC"
Animated fill-frame zoom.
Each segment is
.IR Z0[-Z1][@CX,CY][:RANGE]
separated by
.BR ; .
.I Z
is 1..8 (1 = no zoom).
.I CX,CY
are normalized 0..1 (default 0.5,0.5).
.I Z0-Z1
animates over the range; a single
.I Z
holds that factor.
Example:
.B \-\-zoom 1-2.4@0.5,0.4:2-4;2.4-1:4-6
.TP
.BI \-\-text " SPEC"
Repeatable overlay.
.I SPEC
is
.IR TEXT[:RANGE][+X+Y] .
Range and position are parsed from the right so the label may contain
colons.
Default origin is 0,0 (top-left), bold white on a transparent
background.
Font, style, size, color, and box apply to every
.B \-\-text
on the command.
.TP
.BI \-\-text-font " PATH"
Font file.
Default is DejaVu Sans Bold if installed
.RI ( /usr/share/fonts/dejavu/DejaVuSans-Bold.ttf ).
.TP
.BI \-\-text-style " bold|regular"
Weight.
Default
.BR bold .
.TP
.BI \-\-text-size " N"
Font size in pixels.
Integer 8..512.
Default 28.
.TP
.BI \-\-text-color " RRGGBB[AA]"
Text fill as 6 or 8 hex digits (optional leading
.BR # ).
Default
.BR FFFFFF .
.TP
.BI \-\-text-bg " RRGGBB[AA]"
Solid box color (6 or 8 hex digits, optional leading
.BR # ).
Omit for a transparent background.
Eight digits include alpha (for example
.B 000000c0
is a translucent black box).
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Worker/encoder threads.
Integer >= 1.
Default: all logical cores.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Extra progress on stderr.
Never
.BR \-v .
.TP
.BI \-\-log-file " PATH"
Also write diagnostics to
.IR PATH .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including help and version.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error or no usable inputs.
.TP
.B 2
One or more inputs failed to process.
.SH EXAMPLES
Whole-clip grayscale (writes
.IR clip_gray.mp4 ):
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-grayscale
.RE
.PP
Explode lossless PNG frames:
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-explode
.RE
.PP
Cut two parts:
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-cut 10-20,1:00-1:30
.RE
.PP
Speed up two windows by 2.75× (audio keeps pitch):
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-speed 2.75:10-20,30-40
.RE
.PP
Crop the centre 640×360:
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-crop 640x360
.RE
.PP
Sequential ops (one graph, no ranges needed):
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-crop 640x360 \-\-grayscale \-\-speed 2
.RE
.PP
Grayscale from five minutes to the end:
.RS
.B vidwizard clip.mp4 \-\-grayscale 5:00-
.RE
.PP
Zoom in, then zoom out:
.RS
.EX
vidwizard clip.mp4 \\
  --zoom '1-2.35@0.50,0.40:2.0-4.0;2.35-1@0.50,0.40:4.0-5.5'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Repeatable text with a time window and a pixel origin:
.RS
.EX
vidwizard clip.mp4 \\
  --text 'Zoom in:2-4' \\
  --text 'Crop:7.5-9.2+196+16'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Catwalk demo (sample output:
.IR examples/catwalk-demo.mp4 ):
.RS
.EX
vidwizard catwalk.mp4 -o cat_demo.mp4 \\
  --zoom='1-2.35@0.50,0.40:2.0-4.0;2.35-1@0.50,0.40:4.0-5.5' \\
  --grayscale=5.5-7.5 \\
  --crop=280x200+180+8:7.5-9.2 \\
  --speed=2.2:14-16 \\
  --reverse=16- \\
  --text 'Zoom in:2-4' \\
  --text 'Zoom out:4-5.5' \\
  --text 'Grayscale:5.5-7.5' \\
  --text 'Crop:7.5-9.2+196+16' \\
  --text 'Speed:14-16' \\
  --text 'Reverse:16-' \\
  --text-bg 000000c0 \\
  --text-size 32
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Video is encoded with libx264 (CRF 16, medium preset, yuv420p).
Audio is AAC.
.B \-\-explode
writes 32-bit RGBA PNG.
.PP
.B \-\-reverse
holds selected frames in RAM.
Prefer short windows on large files.
